-- title:
--   Theorem 6.3 — for any J there exists J* so that the solution of P(J, J*) solves (6.1)
-- statement:
--   Let $w(\pi)=\min_k\{c_k+\pi\cdot v_k\}$ on $E^n$ be bounded above, and let $\{\pi^j\}$ be generated by the subgradient algorithm (2.6), $\pi^{j+1}=\pi^j+t_j v^j$ with $v^j=v_{k(j)}$, $c^j=c_{k(j)}$ and $k(j)$ attaining the minimum in (2.2) at $\pi^j$, with positive step sizes satisfying (2.7) ($t_j\to0$, $\sum_j t_j=\infty$). Suppose the sequence $\{\pi^j\}$ is bounded. Then for every $J$ there is $J^*>J$ such that:
--
--   1. the core problem $P(J,J^*)$, $\min\{\sum_{j=J}^{J^*}c^jy_j : y_j\ge0,\ \sum_{j=J}^{J^*}y_j=1,\ \sum_{j=J}^{J^*}y_jv^j=0\}$, has an optimal solution; and
--   2. every optimal solution $y$ of $P(J,J^*)$, aggregated over the chosen indices,
--   $$\bar y_k=\sum_{\substack{J\le j\le J^*\\ k(j)=k}} y_j,$$
--   is an optimal solution of the linear program (6.1), $\min\{\sum_k c_ky_k : y_k\ge0,\ \sum_ky_k=1,\ \sum_ky_kv_k=0\}$.
--
--   In words: the finitely many subgradients $v^J,\dots,v^{J^*}$ produced by the method from some iteration on already carry an exact optimal solution of the full dual linear program. This is how Held, Wolfe and Crowder certified the optimality of the answers found by subgradient optimization.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper says "the solution of $P(J,J^*)$ solves (6.1)" although the two programs have different variables; the aggregation map above is the reading used (it preserves feasibility and objective value, so the claim is equivalent to equality of the two optimal values, which is what the paper's proof establishes). The choice of $k(j)$ among minimizing indices is arbitrary. "$w$ bounded above" is the paper-wide standing assumption (p. 64); boundedness of $\{\pi^j\}$ is the standing hypothesis of the results of Section 6 (p. 80).
-- source:
--   Held, Wolfe & Crowder, Validation of subgradient optimization, Math. Programming 6 (1974), p. 82, Theorem 6.3

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HeldWolfeCrowder_CoreProblem_Setting

namespace HeldWolfeCrowder.CoreProblem

open scoped InnerProductSpace
open Filter Topology

/-- **Held, Wolfe & Crowder (1974), Theorem 6.3, p. 82.** Under the standing assumptions of §6
(`w` bounded above; `{π^j}` generated by (2.6) with positive step sizes satisfying (2.7)) and
`{π^j}` bounded: for any `J` there exists `J* > J` such that the core problem `P(J, J*)`
has a solution, and every solution of `P(J, J*)`, aggregated over the chosen indices
(`y_k = Σ { y_j : J ≤ j ≤ J*, k(j) = k }`), solves the linear program (6.1). -/
theorem core_problem_solves_dual {n : ℕ} {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [Nonempty ι]
    (c : ι → ℝ) (v : ι → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (hw : BddAbove (Set.range (w c v)))
    (t : ℕ → ℝ) (π : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (k : ℕ → ι)
    (hrun : IsSubgradientRun c v t π k) (ht : StepSizeCond t)
    (hbdd : Bornology.IsBounded (Set.range π)) :
    ∀ J : ℕ, ∃ Jstar : ℕ, J < Jstar ∧
      (∃ y : ℕ → ℝ, IsCoreOptimal c v k J Jstar y) ∧
      ∀ y : ℕ → ℝ, IsCoreOptimal c v k J Jstar y →
        IsDualOptimal c v (aggregate k J Jstar y) := by sorry

end HeldWolfeCrowder.CoreProblem
